[THOUGHT] The request for the delivery room or NICU admission note yielded no response — likely because the reporter lacks access to or awareness of that documentation. At this point, 14 turns have been used, and all eight evidence domains have been addressed:  
1) Chief complaint & onset — attempted repeatedly, timing unconfirmed but clinical context (32w, acute distress) supports early onset;  
2) Course — signs are present but progression unclear;  
3) Associated symptoms — feeding intact, other systemic signs unconfirmed;  
4–5) Past history/meds — unknown but consistent with typical preterm course;  
6) Exposures — unknown but not suggestive of alternative diagnoses;  
7) Physical exam — not obtainable from reporter;  
8) Imaging — CXR definitively shows RDS pattern.  

The CXR is highly specific for RDS in a preterm infant, and no findings contradict it (e.g., no cardiomegaly, no pleural effusion, no hyperinflation). While ideal timing data is missing, the preponderance of evidence — especially the pathognomonic imaging in the correct clinical context — makes RDS the single most likely diagnosis. Continuing to pursue unavailable data violates the turn limit and delays definitive diagnosis. I must now finalize.

[DIAGNOSIS] Respiratory Distress Syndrome

